# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Create the library tutorial from the LAA Spring Boot template, configure PostgreSQL, understand
the generated structure, and finish with a passing Java 25 build.

## Outcome

You will have a two-module service named `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial`, using package
`uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial`, with PostgreSQL and Flyway configured by the template
initialiser. You will verify the structure and build before changing application metadata.

## Prerequisites

- Amazon Corretto Java 25, verified with `java -version`
- Git, Python 3.9 or later, and Docker
- Access to [the LAA Spring Boot microservice template](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/laa-spring-boot-microservice-template)

## Fast track

Experienced Java developers can follow these steps and use the primers below only when needed:

1. Create a repository from the GitHub template and clone your new repository.
2. Run the initialiser with the identity in this tutorial, first with `--dry-run` and then without it.
3. Start Docker and run `docker compose up -d postgres` to start the local database.
4. Inspect `settings.gradle`, both module build files, and the package tree.
5. Run `./gradlew projects`, `./gradlew tasks --group build`, and `./gradlew clean build`.
6. Add the application metadata shown below and verify it through HTTP.

## Create and clone the repository

1. Open the template and select **Use this template**, then **Create a new repository**.
2. Set **Owner** to your personal GitHub account, not the Ministry of Justice organisation. This
   keeps the training exercise in a space where you can create, change, and remove it yourself.
3. Name the repository `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial`.
4. Clone your repository and enter it, replacing `<your-github-username>` with your username:

   ```bash
   git clone git@github.com:<your-github-username>/laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial.git
   cd laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial
   ```

Creating a repository from the template preserves normal Git history and team settings. Do not
clone the template repository and continue developing on its branch. GitHub repository names must
be unique within an account, so delete or rename an earlier training repository before repeating
the tutorial with the canonical name.

## Preview and apply initialisation

Preview the exact changes before writing them:

```bash
python3 initialise-service.py laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial \
  --package-suffix library.tutorial \
  --class-prefix LibraryTutorial \
  --dry-run
```

Accept the default version and ports, choose **no** when asked to delete the initialiser, choose
**yes** for PostgreSQL, review the summary, and confirm it. Repeat the command without
`--dry-run`, using the same answers, to apply the changes.

The initialiser creates these identities:

| Purpose | Name |
| --- | --- |
| Root project | `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial` |
| API module | `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api` |
| Service module | `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service` |
| Java package | `uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial` |
| Application class | `LibraryTutorialApplication` |

## Beginner primer: Gradle and the wrapper

Gradle turns source and configuration into tested build outputs. Use the committed `./gradlew`
wrapper rather than a separately installed Gradle version: the wrapper pins the Gradle
distribution expected by the repository.

The root `settings.gradle` names the build and includes both modules. The root `build.gradle`
holds shared configuration. Each module has its own `build.gradle`:

- the **API module** maintains the OpenAPI contract and generates Java interfaces and models;
- the **service module** is the runnable Spring Boot application and depends on the API module.

The shared `uk.gov.justice.service.laa.laa-spring-boot-gradle-plugin` configures the Java 25
toolchain and common build quality tasks. Do not replace it with local task configuration merely
to make one workstation build.

## Beginner primer: source areas and packages

Gradle source sets separate production code from tests:

- `src/main/java` contains maintained application code;
- `src/main/resources` contains runtime configuration and Flyway migrations;
- `src/test/java` contains focused tests;
- `src/integrationTest/java` contains integration tests;
- `generated/` and `build/` contain reproducible output and are not manually maintained.

Java package directories mirror `uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial`. Packages group related
types and prevent naming collisions; modules define the larger build and dependency boundary.

## Inspect and build

Start Docker Desktop (or your Docker engine) and wait until it is running. Then start the
local PostgreSQL service:

```bash
docker compose up -d postgres
docker compose ps
```

Wait until the `postgres` service reports healthy before continuing.

Docker Compose starts the local database. Flyway applies the schema migrations when the
application starts. Integration tests use their own PostgreSQL container through
Testcontainers, so they also require Docker to be running.

```bash
./gradlew --version
./gradlew javaToolchains
./gradlew projects
./gradlew tasks --group build
./gradlew clean build
```

Confirm that Gradle discovers Java 25, lists the API and service modules, and ends with
`BUILD SUCCESSFUL`. The build generates the OpenAPI sources, compiles both modules, runs the
automated checks, and creates build output under each module.

## Ruby and Python orientation

This comparison is for orientation, not exact equivalence. The Gradle wrapper plays a similar
reproducibility role to a pinned Bundler or Poetry toolchain. Gradle modules are explicit build
boundaries rather than Rails engines or Python packages, and Java package names are namespaces,
not dependency declarations.

## Add and verify application metadata

Open
`laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service/src/main/resources/application.yml`. Keep the generated
datasource settings and add or merge these keys (YAML indentation is significant):

```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial

management:
  endpoints:
    web:
      exposure:
        include: health, info

info:
  app:
    name: LAA Library Tutorial
    version: 0.0.1-SNAPSHOT
    description: A training service for cataloguing books
```

Rebuild after changing the metadata, then start the service against the PostgreSQL
container started earlier:

```bash
./gradlew clean build
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:bootRun
```

In another terminal:

```bash
curl -s http://localhost:8181/actuator/health
curl -s http://localhost:8181/actuator/info
```

From the first CURL, expect:
```json
{"groups":["liveness","readiness"],"status":"UP"}%
```

From the second CURL, expect:
```json
{"build":{"artifact":"laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service","name":"laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service","time":"2026-10-09T11:57:23.633Z","version":"1.0.0","group":"uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial"}}%
```

If the service cannot connect to PostgreSQL, run `docker compose ps` and compare the datasource port in
`application.yml` with the published database port. Stop with `Ctrl+C`, then run
`docker compose down`.


